# R12.4 API Trial Plan

element-root parallax and free-mode autoplay restart are still being handled as part of the same interface review for now, but after rechecking, they can no longer be described as the same module-level issue. The first task is to restore the actual calling pattern and the user-visible result; the second requires checking the platforms and versions involved against the current build configuration and mapping any gaps to specific configuration files. Both happen to go through the Swiper class, but that does not mean they share runtime state.

The entry point examined here is `src/types/swiper-class.d.ts`. The file's visible adjacencies are primarily type imports:

```ts
import type { SwiperOptions } from './swiper-options.d.ts';
import type { CSSSelector, SwiperModule } from './shared.d.ts';
import type { SwiperEvents } from './swiper-events.d.ts';
import type { A11yMethods } from './modules/a11y.d.ts';
```

The assessment at that point was still that if both changes require the caller to express intent through an instance, we could first sketch a shared class interface and then have parallax and autoplay consume it separately. This would avoid making callers look for separate entry points in each of the element root and free-mode branches. The cost is also direct: a public field added for review convenience would be hard to retract later, and the actual boundary of restart has not yet been proven by the type file.

SWP-207 serves as the recheck entry point for element-root parallax. SWP-223 records only the platforms, versions, and configuration gaps for free-mode autoplay restart. The proposal does not turn the check results for the two cards into a single pass criterion.

Provisional prototyping sequence: write the invocation examples first, then decide whether the declaration can go in the Swiper class; if the restart for free-mode can only be determined by the module's internal timing, do not add the corresponding public entry point. If the two examples ultimately share only a name—not the invoked object or the withdrawal mechanism—the combined proposal should be split.

